   Perhaps, but remember, you had virtually every item essential to
   winning the game plus an intrinsic -38 AC.  If you've ever played a
   game where you can't find seven candles, you'll see how 'easy' the
   game is then!  Essentially, high level characters are meant to be
   powerful--after all, it's not as if some wimp 10th level 3 int
   barbarian can be a demigod(dess).  So I have no problem with the
   relative power of high level characters.  As well, you probably
   knew a lot about the endgame and the subtle methods of death that
   usually kill off high-level chars--think of the poor novice who
   finally has a good game, only to be killed in the elemental planes
   due to ignorance.  Want to make the game nearly impossible to win
   without reading rec.games.hack?  I don't.

I tried to make it clear that I would like to see no more of the nasty
probabilistic death traps that kill you unless you know beforehand how
to avoid them (after all, I wasn't too happy to find out about krakens
the hard way with a previous character).  Ideally, and I know it would
be difficult to implement, increased challenges for high level
characters should be the kinds of things that can be dealt with "at
the time" using some skill and care, as opposed to essentially
depending on prior knowledge of arcane spoilers.  Also, by high level
character, I mean not only a high level, but also posession of all the
useful items, which eventually happens [unless one has a thing against
polypiling].  In fact, the items are far more important than mere
levels.  E.g. a luckstone enables one to obtain a fast +13 to hit,
which is equivalent to a lot of levels, while the monsters you face
are generated based on your level, not how well you hit.

   >Finally, the bell of opening needs to be changed --- either
   >summon lots of pit fiends, balrogs and nalfeshnees along with the
   >undead, or skip the summoning entirely.

   A large part of the power of high level characters is due to a cursed
   Bell of Opening.  Having a lot of levels helps you to hit monsters, to
   make your saving throw against petrification, and so forth.  The extra
   hit points don't hurt either!  This is the one aspect of the game that
   needs work.  Some demigod around here ascended with 962 hit points.

If you haven't genocided cockatrices, it's crazy to rely on saving
throws against petrification.  That what lizards are for.  In any case
it's ridiculous for a character to be able to get from level 14 to 30
with no real effort, when getting to level 14 is nontrivial.  The most
surprising message I saw in the game with Onan was "welcome to level
18" (or 19, I forget), this level gain not being due to a potion or
wraith corpse.  I didn't really think it was possible to gain a level
past 14 by killing monsters, and for sure it's basically impossible
after level 20.

   >Having a rock mole eat the bell is a rather ridiculous way to be 
   >permanently screwed. 

   This is why you can wish for artifacts. :)

If you wish for a bell of opening, a candelabrum of invocation or the
book of the dead, you won't get the item.  If you don't believe me,
try it out in explore mode (not wizard mode).

   It seems that Mr. Lodenkamper wishes to make the level of
   difficulty more even throughout the game, but I must (respectfully)
   disagree, seeing as I've had three potential demigods go awry
   because of some silly reason or another.  Once your character is
   past a certain point, he deserves to win unless you spaz horribly.
   If the level of difficulty is the same throughout, it seems random
   chance will dictate whether a given character will ascend or not.

Again, I'm not fond of sneaky death traps, but surely there must be
some way of making the post-castle potion of the game more of a
challenge, while making it a little easier for low level characters to
survive. 

Another (rather nasty) possibility along these lines is to have
reading a scroll of gold detection while confused show all traps
*except* magic portals, which would make the elemental levels into
more significant obstacles.

